UNIT directive_spans
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@9e2245be5f42 cli/lsp/pr-review.mjs spans and gitLine, over cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse in its default (legacy) mode
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG directive_spans(text: text) -> list[tuple[i64, i64]]
ENVELOPE Any well-formed text (no lone surrogate), the empty text included. Every such text is in scope and none throws.
  Non-text input is out of the envelope.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION For a SIR text, the line spans of its directives in GIT line numbers, the numbers GitHub shows and a diff's
  hunks use. The result has one pair [start, end] per git line on which at least one directive starts, in ascending
  order. end is the git line before the next such line, or, for the last pair, the text's last git line. The review
  intersects these spans with a diff to place a note on the first changed line of a changed item. Which lines are
  directive lines is the SIR parser's own rule, judged on PARSER lines and restated in full by R1 to R7 and TABLE LINE.
  Comments, indented continuations, blank lines and column-0 lines that are not directive-shaped never start a span.
  They fall inside the span of the directive above them, or in no span when they come before the first directive.
BEHAVIOR
R1 PARSER LINES. The text is cut into parser lines at every parser break (PINNED LINE_BREAK), scanning left to right, so
  CR LF is one break and never two. No other character breaks a parser line: LINE SEPARATOR, PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR, NEXT
  LINE, form feed and vertical tab are content. Parser lines are judged one by one by R2 to R6.
R2 NORMALIZE. Each parser line is judged after two edits. First, every U+FEFF inside the line's leading run of SPACE and
  U+FEFF characters is removed, and the spaces and tabs of that run stay in their order. A U+FEFF after the first other
  character is content. Second, the line's trailing run of SPACE characters is removed.
R3 SKIP. A normalized line is not a directive line when it is empty, when its first character is a SPACE (an indented
  continuation), or when its first character is "#" (a comment; the parser keeps comments, and spans drops them).
R4 NAME. Otherwise the line's NAME is its text up to its first SPACE (excluded), or the whole line when it has none.
  Only SPACE separates. Any other whitespace or invisible character (no-break space, U+3000, U+200B, U+2060, U+FEFF
  after the first character, LINE SEPARATOR, NEXT LINE, form feed, vertical tab) is part of the NAME. So a name glued to
  its arguments by such a character is never a directive, and neither is a line that starts with one.
R5 COLON. When the NAME is PINNED NAME followed by exactly one ":" at its end, that ":" is dropped. The one exception is
  the NAME "CITED:", which keeps its colon. A NAME with two colons, or with characters after the colon, is not changed.
R6 SHAPE. The line is a directive line exactly when its NAME (after R5) matches PINNED NAME and is not exactly "CITED".
  Whether the name is known does not matter. Unknown names count, and so do one-letter words of column-0 prose ("A",
  "I"), R-numbers of any length, and "EARNED" and "ANCHOR" at column 0. Only "CITED" is excluded: it is an attribute,
  and a column-0 "CITED" line continues the directive above it. Nothing after the NAME matters (arguments, quotes, a
  trailing comment, attributes), and the parse mode does not change the directive set.
R7 GIT LINES. The git line of a parser line is 1 plus the number of git breaks (PINNED GIT_BREAK) among the parser breaks
  before it. A lone CR breaks a parser line but does not advance the git line, so a lone CR's two sides share one git
  line. LAST, the text's last git line, is 1 plus the number of LF characters in the text (those inside CR LF
  included), minus 1 when the text ends with LF, and never less than 1. A final LF adds no line; a final lone CR adds
  no line either. The empty text has LAST 1.
R8 SPANS. Let S1 < S2 < … < Sk be the DISTINCT git lines (R7) of the directive lines (R2 to R6). The result is
  [Si, S(i+1) − 1] for each i < k, then [Sk, LAST]. With no directive line the result is the empty list. Directives on
  one git line (parser lines separated only by lone CRs) share ONE pair. Every end is at least its start,
  and Sk never exceeds LAST, since a directive line is never the empty line after a final LF. CITED FIDELITY package —
  the real function keys a map by git line, and a later directive on the same git line replaces the earlier entry's
  end but keeps its place. Only the last directive's end on each git line survives.
R9 ATTRIBUTION. Every line from a directive's git line up to the next pair's start belongs to that pair. That covers its
  continuations, blank lines, comment lines (even one written about the NEXT directive), column-0 prose and glued-name
  lines. CITED FIDELITY package — kept by ruling. Strict mode reports the comment, column-0 prose and glued-name lines
  as errors. A directive-shaped column-0 word (R6) is only a warning there, and it starts its own pair.
TABLE LINE — a parser line after R2 (NORMALIZE); the first row that applies decides (R3 to R6)
  empty (blank, only spaces and tabs, only U+FEFF marks)                                         | not a directive (R3)
  first character a space or tab, including after U+FEFF removal                                 | not a directive: continuation (R3)
  first character "#"                                                                            | not a directive: comment (R3)
  NAME after R5 matches PINNED NAME and is not "CITED"                                           | DIRECTIVE (R6)
  otherwise: a lowercase, digit, "-", "_", non-ASCII or invisible first character; "CITED"; "CITED:"; "A::"; "UNIT:x"; a glued separator | not a directive (R4 to R6)
PINNED grammar LINE_BREAK = CR LF | CR | LF CITED R1 R7. CR = U+000D, LF = U+000A, and the longest alternative wins at each position.
PINNED grammar GIT_BREAK = CR LF | LF CITED R7. These are the parser breaks that advance the git line; a lone CR is not one of them.
PINNED charset SPACE = { U+0020, U+0009 } CITED R2 R3 R4. This is the parser's one whitespace rule, and no other character is in it.
PINNED grammar NAME = UPPER ( UPPER | DIGIT | "-" )* CITED R5 R6. UPPER = the 26 ASCII capital letters, DIGIT = the 10 ASCII digits, and there is no case folding.
INVARIANT starts are strictly increasing, each pair but the last ends one line before the next pair's start, and the
  last pair ends at LAST.
NOTE No rust variant. Cause: domain — the review system ships as JavaScript (rdv review and the rederive/sir-review GitHub
  Action); there is no Rust consumer.
ORACLE value -> oracles/directive_spans.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 c8eb9a9437e8a74896c49c4c9f3c4f49dc5cfa1dc9b82345d5bf5df309043479
SCOPE IN: R1 to R9 over any well-formed text. OUT: the parser's AST contents, diagnostics, attributes and comments; how
  the review intersects the spans with a diff; the gitLine map for lines that are not directive lines; non-text input.
PROVENANCE rederive@9e2245be5f42 (MIT), cli/lsp/pr-review.mjs export spans (lines 43 to 45) and gitLine (line 40), on
  cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse and sirLines. The seam selfhost/seams/directive_spans.mjs returns the Map's entries, in
  insertion order, as a list of pairs.
